>
  <p>Zaaheen is an independent AI consultancy based in Dubai that assesses businesses across the
     Gulf, on site and role by role. We are not a holder, issuer or partner of the Dubai AI Seal or
     of any scheme on this page. The official site, <a href={SEAL}>dub.ai/en/ai-seal</a>, is the
     authority on its rules; we last checked it on 26 November 2026.</p>

  <section id="what-is-it">
    <h2>What is the Dubai AI Seal?</h2>
    <p>The Dubai AI Seal is a verification system run by the Dubai Centre for Artificial
       Intelligence (DCAI), in an initiative overseen by the Dubai Future Foundation. It was launched in January 2025
       under the directives of Sheikh Hamdan bin Mohammed, Crown Prince of Dubai, as part of the
       Dubai Universal Blueprint for AI (<a href={APRIL_2025}>Dubai Media Office, 22 April 2025</a>).</p>
    <p>Its job is to separate genuine AI providers from companies that only use the word. DCAI
       says the seal goes only to businesses that "genuinely provide AI technology", and lists
       "protection from irrelevant suppliers and AI washing" as a benefit for the government and
       private companies that buy from them (<a href={SEAL}>DCAI</a>). Each certified company gets
       a seal showing its tier and a unique serial number, which anyone can check on the seal's
       website.</p>
    <p>The first holders were presented at Dubai AI Week in April 2025. By 15 May 2025, 325
       companies operating in Dubai had applied, representing 77 international offices, and e&amp; and
       IBM were among the first awarded the top tier (<a href={MAY_2025}>Dubai Media Office, 15 May
       2025</a>). We found no published count of how many companies hold the seal today.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="who-can-apply">
    <h2>Who can apply for the Dubai AI Seal?</h2>
    <p>Companies that provide AI, not companies that use it. The scheme is open to local,
       regional and international companies licensed to operate in Dubai that offer AI
       products or services, or build AI into what they sell (<a href={MAY_2025}>Dubai Media Office</a>).
       DCAI says AI businesses of any size can apply.</p>
    <p>The National reported five categories of activity: AI consulting, development,
       infrastructure, integration and ancillary services
       (<a href={NATIONAL}>The National, 19 August 2025</a>). A restaurant group using an AI
       chatbot, or a logistics firm using AI to plan routes, is a user of AI and is not who the
       seal is for. If your business sells a product or service with AI inside it, you may be
       eligible, and DCAI decides.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="tiers">
    <h2>What do the Dubai AI Seal tiers mean?</h2>
    <p>There are six tiers: E, D, C, B, A and S, with S marking the highest impact on Dubai's AI
       economy. DCAI places each company using its Dubai AI Business Activity Classification
       System, based on the nature of its activities and services, its current and future
       projects, and its economic contribution to the AI sector (<a href={SEAL}>DCAI</a>).</p>
    <p>The assessment looks at the nature of the company's AI work, the number of specialised AI
       engineers it employs, its current and upcoming projects, and its partnerships with
       government and the private sector (<a href={APRIL_2025}>Dubai Media Office</a>). DCAI has
       not published what each tier requires, how long a seal lasts or how renewal works. Any page
       that gives exact tier thresholds is guessing; ask DCAI.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="how-to-apply">
    <h2>How do you get the Dubai AI Seal?</h2>
    <p>You apply online, and it costs nothing. DCAI describes three steps (<a href={SEAL}>DCAI</a>):</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Apply.</strong> Submit the online form at dub.ai/en/ai-seal with your business
          information.</li>
      <li><strong>Classification.</strong> The DCAI team reviews the application and places the
          company in a tier.</li>
      <li><strong>Seal issued.</strong> Approved companies receive a personalised seal with their
          tier and serial number, and may show it on their websites and marketing.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Be careful with anyone offering to "get you the seal" for a fee. The application is free
       and goes straight to DCAI.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="government">
    <h2>Do you need the seal for Dubai government AI contracts?</h2>
    <p>In practice, yes. Dubai's Media Office said in April 2025 that any technology company
       wishing to take part in government AI projects in Dubai "must first obtain" the seal
       (<a href={APRIL_2025}>Dubai Media Office</a>). On 19 October 2025, Dubai Finance urged
       government entities to restrict AI-related service and consultancy contracts to companies
       holding it, and to tell consultancies and AI providers that they need it
       (<a href={OCT_2025}>Dubai Media Office</a>).</p>
    <p>That rule is about Dubai government buyers. We found no published requirement that private
       companies, other emirates or federal bodies use it, so for a private business it is a
       useful check, not an obligation.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="buyers">
    <h2>What does the seal mean if your business is buying AI?</h2>
    <p>It answers one question well: is this supplier a real AI company, active in Dubai? It does
       not answer the question that decides whether your project works: does this supplier's
       product fit the way your people actually work?</p>
    <p>That second question is where most Gulf businesses are thin. Roland Berger's Middle East
       report of 10 February 2026 found most GCC organisations plan to use AI, while far fewer have
       the data strategy or the governance to run it:</p>

    <BarChart
      id="chart-gcc-ai-governance"
      title="GCC organisations: AI plans against data and governance, 2026"
      measure="Share of GCC organisations"
      max={100}
      bars={[
        { label: 'AI in strategic plans', value: 80, note: '% (nearly)' },
        { label: 'Enterprise data strategy', value: 33, note: '% (one third)' },
        { label: 'AI ethics or compliance board', value: 28, note: '%' },
      ]}
      source={{ text: 'Roland Berger, Middle East report "4 in 5 GCC organizations have an AI strategy", 10 February 2026. Sample size not stated. "Only one-third" is drawn at 33%.', href: ROLAND_BERGER }}
    />

    <p>A seal on a supplier's website cannot close that gap for you. Before you compare sealed
       suppliers, it pays to know four things about your own business:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Where AI would actually help.</strong> Which tasks read, write, sort or answer at
          volume, and which are better served by a simple rule or form. Our guide to
          <a href="/learn/ai-vs-automation/">AI vs automation</a> explains the difference.</li>
      <li><strong>How the work is done today.</strong> A mapped process, with its hand-offs and
          waiting points, is the brief a supplier needs. Without it, you are buying from a demo.</li>
      <li><strong>Where a person stays in charge.</strong> Which decisions are too costly to get
          wrong to hand to a system, and who checks the output.</li>
      <li><strong>What data the tool would touch.</strong> Which records it reads, where they
          live, and who in your team owns them.</li>
    </ul>

    <MemoryFlow
      id="flow-seal-and-fit"
      from="AI supplier pitch"
      to="Right supplier"
      fact="Seal checked, fit checked"
      hub="Your own map"
      hubNote="where AI fits, who decides"
      saved="Fit confirmed"
      caption="The seal tells you a supplier is a genuine AI company. Your own map of the work tells you whether its product fits your business."
    />

    <p>With those answers, the seal becomes a useful second filter: you shortlist suppliers that
       fit your work, then check their serial numbers on the DCAI site. Projects that skip the first
       step are the ones that stall, as our guide to
       <a href="/learn/why-ai-projects-fail/">why AI projects fail</a> sets out.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="gulf">
    <h2>Are there similar AI certification schemes elsewhere in the Gulf?</h2>
    <p>A few, and they work differently. In Saudi Arabia, the Saudi Data and AI Authority (SDAIA)
       awards AI ethics badges to organisations based on their maturity in applying its AI Ethics
       Principles. Press reports describe five levels and one-year validity, and report that more
       than 20 companies received badges at the UNESCO Global Forum on the Ethics of AI in Riyadh
       in 2026. The level
       names differ between reports, so check SDAIA directly.</p>
    <p>At federal level in the UAE, the Cyber Security Council announced a national AI test and
       validation lab in May 2026 to assess AI models, agents and applications, with a national
       certification mark for systems that pass. That certifies AI systems, not the businesses using
       them. We found no comparable company scheme in Abu Dhabi or Qatar at the time of writing.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="prepare">
    <h2>Where does Zaaheen fit if your business is buying AI?</h2>
    <p>To be clear first: our assessment does not get anyone a seal, and it is not a certification
       of any AI system. What it does is give a business that wants to use AI the groundwork a good
       supplier decision needs.</p>
    <p>In <a href="/consulting/">our AI Readiness Assessment</a>, someone from our team comes on
       site and goes through the business department by department, sitting with the people doing
       the work and timing each step. Every process becomes a map, and each team gets one of four
       verdicts: AI now, Automate without AI, Fix the process first, or Leave as it is. For every
       recommendation we give the effort, a cost range, the hours it saves each month, when it pays
       back, and where a person must stay in charge.</p>
    <p>We are independent: we sell, resell and build no AI tools, and take no commissions from any
       provider, so the report is a neutral brief you can take to any supplier, sealed or not. If you
       are still weighing whether to start, read
       <a href="/learn/ai-readiness-assessment-uae/">what an AI readiness assessment in the UAE
       covers</a>, or book <a href="/consulting/">a free 30-minute call</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="faq">
    <h2>Common questions about the Dubai AI Seal</h2>

    <h3>Is the Dubai AI Seal free?</h3>
    <p>Yes. DCAI says the service is free of charge, and you apply directly on its website at
       dub.ai/en/ai-seal.</p>

    <h3>Can a company that only uses AI get the Dubai AI Seal?</h3>
    <p>Not on the published rules. The seal is for companies licensed in Dubai that provide AI
       products or services, or build AI into what they offer. A business that uses AI tools
       internally is a buyer of AI, not a provider.</p>

    <h3>How do I check if a supplier has the Dubai AI Seal?</h3>
    <p>Ask for its serial number and check it on the Dubai AI Seal website, which DCAI says can
       verify any seal. A logo on a website alone proves nothing.</p>

    <h3>How long does the Dubai AI Seal last?</h3>
    <p>DCAI has not published a validity period or renewal process. Ask DCAI directly before
       relying on an old seal.</p>

    <h3>Is the Dubai AI Seal the same as a free AI certification from the Dubai government?</h3>
    <p>No. The seal is a classification for companies. Free AI courses and certificates for
       individuals are separate programmes, and completing one does not give a company the seal.</p>

    <h3>Does Zaaheen hold the Dubai AI Seal?</h3>
    <p>No. We are an independent consultancy and have no role in the scheme. We assess private
       businesses that want to use AI, and the seal's own site is the authority on its rules.</p>
  </section>
</Learn>
